we're trying to figure  out new orleans and we're the first city  to ever have to tackle these questions  so what's the tension between regulation  autonomy and so we've totally  decentralized educational programming  educators on the ground get to make  decisions about what they think is best  for kids and we've totally decentralized  choice family is going to pick what  schools are right for them over the past  four to five years we've put in a bunch  of regulations to help ensure that when  you're going to choice we also have  equity so we have a centralized  enrollment system we have a centralized  expulsion system charter schools cannot  just kick kids out this is this is how  we want with the regulatory cream the  idea that you can distinguish choice  from equity I think is a nifty  rhetorical trick but I'm not sure it's  as simple as those folks you talk to  would like it to be  although the size and scope of New  Orleans educational bureaucracy is a  small fraction of what it used to be the  complex structure of public school  governance remains a serious obstacle to  continued reform the challenge of  navigating the city's tangled web of  authorities while educating the city's  43,000 students is taken on by dedicated  principles like Julie louse of the  Harriet Tubman charter school in Algiers  well I mean it's very similar to a lot  of other schools Harriet Tubman was a  failing school in 2011 within two years  we moved it from failing to a see the  autonomy is the number one reason why I  feel like I can make a difference here  right so I have autonomy over my staff  and my curriculum and my program my  schedule in a day and all that kind of  stuff if I didn't have that I wouldn't  be able to do anything the balance  between regulation and autonomy came to  a head over the issue of student  discipline until last year New Orleans  charter schools set their own policies  for disciplining unruly students but  when a few schools were found to have  unusually high rates of expulsion 'he's  parents got angry parents teens and  activists speak out against what they  call oppressive conditions at the  charter schools complaints range from  mass suspensions they were just called  and said we're suspending him and we're  going to send him home on a public RTA  and give him a bus token the state was  quick to intervene taking control of all  student disciplinary procedures in New  Orleans but later that year Louisiana's  own state investigation of school  expulsion painted a very different  picture New Orleans has followed the  national trend in every year they've  been measured charter schools have lower  rates of expelling students than  traditional public schools elsewhere in  the state and by some measures  explosions actually increased after the  state took over the process of  disciplining students but if parents are  unhappy with their school's expulsion  policy why are they protesting in the  streets why don't they just exercise  their right to school choice and  transfer their child to another school  I've been trying to get on another  school to pull out of here that's hard  because it's not so far in the school  year that I can't just pull out and put  us somewhere else I wish I could because  a new regulation enacted last year  prevents students from transferring  to another school after six weeks into  the semester so we used to have a system  where any day in the school year as  school a child could transfer out and  what we found is that led to higher  mobility rates and you could also get  into tip first half of a kid gets a  detention and then they pull out of the  school and that can be destabilizing  towards the system of schools believe me  I there have been parents who've been in  my school who really want to switch  after six weeks and I want to support  them because they are so unhappy the  mid-year switches are not only stressful  for kids and families but they're  stressful for schools to get that many  kids in the middle of the year coming  from other schools at some point we have  to shut the enrollment process down and  say okay we're done with their moment  for the year now the next chance is next  year that does take away from some of  our autonomy but I think in the long run  it's better for kids and for schools  it's something we have to be very  careful of a system like this could be  undermined by a death by a thousand  regulatory cuts what makes me optimistic  that that won't happen is first I think  the political class is starting to  understand why this system works and I  hope that that's getting ingrained in  the DNA of who we are as a city second  is you have a pretty big constituency at  this point that I hope will fight for  their rights part of the challenge is  how do you say look these are reasonable  regulations but we don't want to go any  further than this what where do we stake  out the no further than this and I think  this is where the charter community has  not done as good a job as it as it  should in New Orleans or anywhere else  for anybody that's criticizing the  public school system and New Orleans by  saying oh you guys think you know  everything we don't we're working hard  at it in an imperfect system but I am  proud of where we are I mean  thirty-three percent of the city passed  tested basic levels in 2004 and now it's  67 just that growth has never been seen  in the history of our country before  we're going to be the first mostly black  city to outperform it's mostly white  state in the history of this country so  I think there's something significant  about to what's happening here and we're  not yet there we're not yet perfect we  aren't in a school system yet but I  think we're on the way
 

